'''Dafar Sare''' (Burawi: ''Dafar Sare'', meaning "Upper Dafar"), officially the '''Dafar Sare Rural Administration''', is a [[Administrative divisions of Burawa|rural administration]] in the [[Dafar]] cultural region of western [[Burawa]]. With an area of 6,404.08 km² and a population of 202,504 as of 2026, it is characterised by its agricultural economy and distinctive regional political culture.

The administration is bordered by [[Dafar Hoose Rural Administration|Dafar Hoose]] to the southwest and [[Qansax Rural Administration|Qansax]] to the north. The seat of government is the town of [[Ead, Dafar Sare|Ead]].

Dafar Sare is notable in Burawi politics as the stronghold of the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]] and its founder, Senator [[Bile Sago]], who has represented the administration in the [[Senate of Burawa]] since 2009. The region is known for its annual [[Sago Harvest Festival]] and its strong regionalist identity within the broader Dafar cultural area.

== Geography ==

Dafar Sare occupies the northeastern portion of the [[Dafar]] region in western [[Burawa]], situated along the border with [[Kijura]]. The terrain is characterised by hilly uplands and mountain slopes, with elevations higher than the surrounding lowland areas of the Dafar region to the southwest.

Dafar Sare shares borders with [[Dafar Hoose Rural Administration|Dafar Hoose]] to the southwest and [[Qansax Rural Administration|Qansax]] to the north. The western border with Kijura follows the elevated ridgeline of the Kurdid Mountains.

=== Climate ===

The administration experiences a {{w|humid subtropical climate}}, with warm summers and mild winters. Annual rainfall averages 800–1,000 mm, concentrated in the winter months. The hilly terrain creates varied microclimates across the administration, with higher elevations receiving greater precipitation.

=== Settlements ===

The administration is divided into [[commune]]s, the largest of which is [[Dafar Sare-1]], the administrative centre. Other significant communes include [[Dafar Sare-2]], [[Dafar Sare-3]], and numerous smaller settlements. The population is dispersed across agricultural holdings, with the majority residing in small villages and farmsteads rather than concentrated urban centres.

