== History ==

=== Formation ===

The Dafar Sare Rural Administration was constituted in 1967 under the [[Administration Reorganization Act (Burawa)|Administration Reorganization Act]], which reorganised Burawa's administrative divisions into the current three-tier system. The administration was carved from the historic [[Sago]] region, which had previously been administered as part of the broader Dafar administrative area.

The name "Dafar Sare" derives from the Burawi words for "Upper Dafar," reflecting the administration's position in the northern portion of the Dafar region.

=== Political developments ===

Historically, Dafar Sare was a stronghold of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]], consistent with the broader Dafar region's conservative and agrarian political character. This began to change in the 2000s with the rise of [[Bile Sago]], a local politician who won election as commune representative for [[Dafar Sare-1]] in 2000.

In 2009, Sago mounted a successful campaign for the Senate seat, defeating the incumbent [[Guuleed Dheere]] of the National Party. His campaign, conducted alongside his wife [[Ubax Sago]]'s campaign for his former commune seat, became known as the "[[Love Tour]]" (Burawi: ''Safarka Jacaylka''). The victory established Dafar Sare as a centre of regionalist politics within Burawa.

Sago was expelled from the National Party in 2016 following a series of controversies, including a widely publicised incident at the [[Sago Harvest Festival]] in which he delivered an intoxicated speech criticising the administration's administrator. In 2020, Sago founded the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]], which has since dominated politics in Dafar Sare and the neighbouring [[Dafar Hoose Rural Administration|Dafar Hoose]].

== Government and politics ==

As a rural administration, Dafar Sare is governed by an administrator who serves a six-year term. The administrator is nominated by the senator and confirmed by a vote of the communes within the administration. The incumbent administrator is [[Abdirizak Dheere]] of the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]], appointed in 2022.

=== Senate representation ===

{{main|Senate of Burawa}}

Dafar Sare holds one seat in the [[Senate of Burawa]]. The incumbent senator is [[Bile Sago]] of the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]], first elected in 2009 and re-elected in 2012, 2015, 2018, 2021, and 2024.

=== Lower House representation ===

{{main|Lower House of Burawa}}

Dafar Sare is divided into two legislative districts for the [[Lower House of Burawa]]. Both seats are held by the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]], reflecting the party's dominance in the administration. The incumbent representatives are:

