=== Political culture ===

Dafar Sare has developed a distinctive political culture centred on regional grievance and Sago nationalism. The [[Sago Revolutionary Party]]'s platform emphasises the interests of the Sago region within the broader Burawi state, criticising what it characterises as neglect by the national government. The party's support base is concentrated among agricultural workers and rural residents who perceive a disconnect between the capital region and the western provinces.

== Economy ==

The economy of Dafar Sare is predominantly agricultural, adapted to the hilly terrain. The higher elevations support pastoral farming and livestock husbandry, particularly sheep and goats, while terraced fields on the slopes permit cultivation of grain and vegetables. The region is known for its production of [[Sago cheese]], a local specialty exported throughout Burawa.

Unlike the metropolitan administrations, Dafar Sare has limited industrial development. Small-scale food processing and agricultural services constitute the primary non-agricultural employment. The administration's population density of approximately 32 persons per km² reflects its rural character and mountainous terrain.

== Culture ==

=== Sago Harvest Festival ===

{{main|Sago Harvest Festival}}

The [[Sago Harvest Festival]] is an annual celebration held in Dafar Sare, marking the conclusion of the harvest season. The festival features agricultural displays, traditional music, and regional cuisine. The festival gained national attention in 2011 when Senator [[Bile Sago]] delivered an impromptu speech while intoxicated, an incident that became part of his political legend.

=== Regional identity ===

Dafar Sare residents maintain a strong regional identity distinct from the broader Dafar cultural area. This identity is characterised by attachment to the Sago name and a sense of separateness from the coastal cities of the Sago-Dare metropolitan area. The [[Sago Revolutionary Party]] has capitalised on and reinforced this regional consciousness.

== Demographics ==

The population of Dafar Sare is 202,504 as of 2026, dispersed across the administration's 6,404.08 km² territory. The population density of 31.6 persons per km² is among the lowest for a rural administration in southern Burawa, reflecting the predominance of agricultural land use.

The majority of residents are ethnically [[Burawi people|Burawi]], with small minorities of [[Kijuran people|Kijuran]] and other groups. The primary language is [[Burawi language|Burawi]], with regional dialects characteristic of the Dafar region.

